    },
    {
      "text": "The first independent safety test of a consumer health AI out of Mount Sinai found that this health AI is under triaging life-threatening emergency 50% of the times.",
      "timestamp_sec": 50,
      "why": "Hard number from an outside evaluation, not a vendor claim."
    },
    {
      "text": "In February, ECRI the patient safety group that hospitals trust to rank their top risks named AI chatbot misuse as the number one health technology hazard of 2026.",
      "timestamp_sec": 104,
      "why": "Anchors the risk framing in an institutional ranking rather than anecdote."
    },
    {
      "text": "Most AI safety failures in health care are not model failures. They are architectural decisions that were made before even a single token was generated.",
      "timestamp_sec": 154,
      "why": "The thesis of the entire talk in two sentences."
    },
    {
      "text": "Two, deterministic rules belong above the model, not inside it. What can never be wrong cannot be left to probability.",
      "timestamp_sec": 154,
      "why": "States the code-above-model principle as a design rule."
    },
    {
      "text": "Launch of your product is where the real risk starts, not where it ends.",
      "timestamp_sec": 154,
      "why": "Compact argument against treating evals as a pre-launch gate."
    },
    {
      "text": "The architecture version strips PHI at the pipeline boundary. At ingestion before it ever reaches the data lake. By the time the data is stored, the PHI is gone.",
      "timestamp_sec": 210,
      "why": "The concrete mechanism behind 'don't policy what you can architect'."
    },
    {
      "text": "You cannot slap on HIPAA on top of, you know, an underlying system or an architecture. You start with it and let the architecture grow around it.",
      "timestamp_sec": 309,
      "why": "Names compliance as a design input rather than a review step."
    },
    {
      "text": "A model is not a guardrail. A model with a system prompt is also not a guardrail. Code that runs above the model is closer.",
      "timestamp_sec": 408,
      "why": "Directly contradicts the common practice of encoding safety rules in system prompts."
    },
    {
      "text": "If the labs themselves don't trust the prompt as a security boundary, neither should you.",
      "timestamp_sec": 408,
      "why": "Turns the labs' published authority hierarchy into an argument against prompt-based safety."
    },
    {
      "text": "If a member mentions self-harm, suicidal ideation, or an acute medical emergency, the system must route to 911 or 988. The model should not even see this turn.",
      "timestamp_sec": 469,
      "why": "The sharpest concrete instance of code running before the model."
    },
    {
      "text": "Anything that touches member data has to check that the right member is at the other end. That's an authentication check. And authentication is a security bound boundary. Prompts are not.",
      "timestamp_sec": 520,
      "why": "Applies the security-boundary argument to identity verification specifically."
    },
    {
      "text": "What actually holds up in production is judges that continuously keep scoring real conversations as they happen. Not a saved golden data set. Live traffic.",
      "timestamp_sec": 581,
      "why": "Takes a side against static golden datasets as the primary eval mechanism."
    },
    {
      "text": "The bottleneck is not the compute, the models, the capability. It's actually having enough people to read the signal and act on it.",
      "timestamp_sec": 687,
      "why": "Names the real scaling constraint, against the usual compute-bound framing."
    },
    {
      "text": "A new failure that you see in production simply means you now have a new judge.",
      "timestamp_sec": 734,
      "why": "A reusable operating rule for turning incidents into monitoring."
    },
    {
      "text": "A bug that lightly annoys 100% of users is way less severe than one that could cause serious harm in 0.1% of cases.",
      "timestamp_sec": 836,
      "why": "Makes the worst-case-over-average severity rule concrete and checkable."
    },
    {
      "text": "You never quietly downgrade a bug just because you can't get to it.",
      "timestamp_sec": 894,
      "why": "Names the specific organizational failure mode the severity rule exists to prevent."
    },
    {
      "text": "Shipping a real safety bug is much worse than delaying for a false alarm.",
      "timestamp_sec": 944,
      "why": "The asymmetry that justifies defaulting to hold on safety issues."
    },
    {
      "text": "Your launch bar is what your org already accepts in production, not what it says it will accept.",
      "timestamp_sec": 992,
      "why": "Revealed vs. stated risk tolerance — the most contestable rule in the framework."
    },
    {
      "text": "Fast follows are committed debt, not an optional backlog.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1052,
      "why": "Reframes a near-universal shipping compromise as an obligation."
    },
    {
      "text": "In a non-deterministic system, the judge is also non-deterministic. Before you trust the score, verify the scorer.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1052,
      "why": "The core discipline of the LLM-judge section, stated as a rule."
    },
    {
      "text": "The rule is always ask, is the judge right before changing the agent's response? Fixing a judge prompt is not cheating. Judges are software, too.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1159,
      "why": "Pushes back on the instinct that editing a judge is gaming the eval."
    },
    {
      "text": "Don't policy what you can architect. Don't prompt what you can code. Don't gate what you can monitor.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1214,
      "why": "The talk's three architecture takeaways compressed into one memorable pattern."
    },
    {
      "text": "We are not building a generic low-stakes chatbot. We are building a system that has to be worthy of someone's health.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1267,
      "why": "The closing justification for accepting slower guardrails-first development."
    }
  ],
